1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
It's mass produced by the billion and handled with the care and attention of fine wine.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
After water, it's the most popular drink on the planet.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Quenching the world's thirst for it has raised and wrecked empires.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
It's a drink of the ancient past and the trendy future.

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, tea on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,240
Tea.

7
00:00:42,241 --> 00:00:47,940
It's a delicate leaf renowned for its ability to stimulate and soothe.

8
00:00:47,941 --> 00:00:53,160
And yet this little leaf has played a stunningly significant role in the history of the planet,

9
00:00:53,161 --> 00:00:58,340
sparking revolution, precipitating war, and drastically reshaping landscapes across the

10
00:00:58,341 --> 00:01:01,800
globe.

11
00:01:01,801 --> 00:01:05,000
The human thirst for tea is phenomenal.

12
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:10,380
We drink around one and a half trillion cups of tea a year, and it remains to this day

13
00:01:10,381 --> 00:01:15,820
a mystery drink, full of paradoxical qualities that are the essence of its appeal.

14
00:01:15,821 --> 00:01:18,000
If you're cold, tea will warm you.

15
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:20,600
If you're warm, tea will cool you.

16
00:01:20,601 --> 00:01:22,940
If you're excited, it will soothe you.

17
00:01:22,941 --> 00:01:25,980
If you're lethargic, it will stimulate you.

18
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:29,840
The history of tea and the taste of tea

19
00:01:29,841 --> 00:01:33,660
and the vast amount of different types of teas

20
00:01:33,661 --> 00:01:37,440
and different manufacturing styles and flavors, aromas, textures, colors,

21
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:41,580
characteristics of tea are such that it speaks to us somehow.

22
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:44,260
And once you're exposed to it, you want to learn more,

23
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:46,320
you want to taste more, you want to experience more.

24
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:50,660
Tea is a major cash crop in dozens of countries around the world,

25
00:01:50,661 --> 00:01:56,180
especially in Indonesia, China, India, Kenya, and Argentina.

26
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:58,640
But if you want to find it in North America,

27
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:04,160
you have to come here to the Charleston Tea Plantation on Wadmula Island, South Carolina.

28
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:11,520
This 127-acre tea farm includes a nursery for developing new bushes

29
00:02:11,521 --> 00:02:18,000
and an on-site factory that converts freshly picked tea leaf into black tea

30
00:02:18,001 --> 00:02:21,540
that is sold throughout the South under the American classic label.

31
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:26,460
South Carolina and Hawaii are the only places in the U.S.

32
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:30,780
that have the kind of semi-tropical, high rainfall climate needed for growing tea.

33
00:02:30,781 --> 00:02:35,460
What tea enjoys is high temperature and high humidity,

34
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,980
and Charleston certainly has that during the summer.

35
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:44,500
So really the tea thrives very well in this location.

36
00:02:44,501 --> 00:02:49,500
The Charleston tea plantation has a long and noble history.

37
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:52,160
Tea bushes can live hundreds of years,

38
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:56,560
and the thousands of bushes here are all seeded and cloned from Chinese plants

39
00:02:56,561 --> 00:02:59,400
that were imported to Charleston over 200 years ago.

40
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:06,660
These plants date back to the first tea plants brought to America back in 1799

41
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:10,440
by a French botanist by the name of André Muchot,

42
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:13,240
who brought these plants from the Far East.

43
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:20,500
The original bushes were planted about an hour's drive from here, just outside of Charleston.

44
00:03:20,501 --> 00:03:27,500
They produced commercial tea until that plantation went out of business in 1915.

45
00:03:27,501 --> 00:03:37,500
The Lipton Tea Company bought the bushes, which it continued to grow on their own, and replanted cuttings here in 1960 as part of a research and development farm.

46
00:03:37,501 --> 00:03:43,500
When Lipton decided to shut the farm down in 1987, the Bigelow Tea Company stepped in.

47
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:47,245
This is a gem. This is a one-of-a-kind thing.

48
00:03:47,246 --> 00:03:50,740
And it was just impossible in our minds to

49
00:03:50,741 --> 00:03:52,740
see this disappear.

50
00:03:52,741 --> 00:03:59,340
The Charleston plantation provides just a fraction of the tea consumed in the U.S.

51
00:03:59,341 --> 00:04:04,820
Americans drink 50 billion servings of tea a year, which sounds like a lot, but is itself

52
00:04:04,821 --> 00:04:08,820
just a fraction of what the rest of the world drinks.

53
00:04:08,821 --> 00:04:11,420
Tea is only the sixth most popular drink in the U.S.

54
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,780
After water, coffee, soft drinks, alcohol, and bottled water.

55
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,160
As the number two drink worldwide,

56
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,160
tea is harvested in gargantuan quantities.

57
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:25,500
Six billion pounds of tea are produced every year.

58
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:28,800
And it takes five pounds of tea, raw leaf tea,

59
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:30,580
to make one pound of finished tea.

60
00:04:30,581 --> 00:04:33,680
That means 30 billion pounds of tea

61
00:04:33,681 --> 00:04:36,240
have to be virtually hand-picked throughout the world.

62
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:50,302
Commercial tea is usually farmed on vast plantations, like this one owned by Unilever in Kenya, that stretches over 27,000 acres, employs 24,000 people, and has

63
00:04:50,303 --> 00:04:52,240
numerous factories on site.

64
00:04:53,760 --> 00:05:04,015
It's also cultivated on thousands of smaller family farms, especially in China, where tea farming began millennia ago, and where tea processing as well as farming is

65
00:05:04,016 --> 00:05:05,880
still frequently done by hand.

66
00:05:05,881 --> 00:05:12,760
There are three major types of tea, black, green, and oolong.

67
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:18,040
But few outside the tea business realize that all three types come from the same plant.

68
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:25,580
Camellia sinensis, which is the only tea plant in the world, and is indigenous to China, India, and Thailand.

69
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:34,980
Like wine, there are an estimated 1,500 varietals of tea, with endless varying qualities based on soil and climate.

70
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,580
And some are more suited to make one kind of tea over another.

71
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:44,620
But the real differences between black, green, and oolong are created after the leaf has been picked.

72
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:52,520
At the Charleston plantation, we're going to turn about 5,000 pounds of freshly picked Camellia sinensis into black tea.

73
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:58,180
The first step in making any kind of tea is called withering.

74
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:03,280
The leaves are placed on a withering bed, where they lie for 18 hours.

75
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:10,400
Freshly plucked tea is 80% moisture, and that percentage has to come down to exactly 68%.

76
00:06:10,401 --> 00:06:19,500
If the moisture were too low, it would not make a fine quality tea because the enzymes have overbuilt inside the leaf.

77
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:29,540
If they're not withered enough, they haven't built to the sufficient amount to give you that full-bodied flavor when you drink a cup of hot tea or iced tea.

78
00:06:29,541 --> 00:06:36,540
Giant fans accelerate the drying process and make sure the leaves wither equally.

79
00:06:36,541 --> 00:06:42,540
The fans alternately blow and suck air for an hour at a time through hundreds of holes in the withering bed.

80
00:06:42,541 --> 00:06:49,540
Without the fans, the leaves in the middle would turn red and spoil.

81
00:06:49,541 --> 00:06:58,540
Traditional tea manufacturing in places like China doesn't require fans because it involves much smaller amounts of tea.

82
00:06:58,541 --> 00:07:07,000
After withering, the leaf at Charleston is conveyed to a shredding machine called a rotovane,

83
00:07:07,001 --> 00:07:11,854
where the tea will be ripped into pieces small enough to fit into a tea bag.

84
00:07:11,855 --> 00:07:12,940
But it's not just

85
00:07:12,941 --> 00:07:14,056
a question of size.

86
00:07:14,057 --> 00:07:18,520
Shredding is crucial because it tears open the cell walls of the leaf, which

87
00:07:18,521 --> 00:07:20,032
allows the tea to oxidize.

88
00:07:20,033 --> 00:07:24,160
There are many ways to break open the cell walls, including a number of

89
00:07:24,161 --> 00:07:28,060
traditional hand rolling methods still popular in China.

90
00:07:28,061 --> 00:07:30,220
The rotovane and other mechanical devices

91
00:07:30,221 --> 00:07:34,240
are widely used when working with large amounts of leaf.

92
00:07:34,241 --> 00:07:38,100
Inside this rotovane, stationary fingers

93
00:07:38,101 --> 00:07:42,300
that we call veins and rotors that go around.

94
00:07:42,301 --> 00:07:44,780
And what it's doing is pressing the T

95
00:07:44,781 --> 00:07:48,520
against these veins to rupture that cell wall

96
00:07:48,521 --> 00:07:50,640
and expose the T juices.

97
00:07:50,641 --> 00:07:52,560
We want every cell wall broken.

98
00:07:52,561 --> 00:07:55,760
and we want those juices exposed to the air.

99
00:07:55,761 --> 00:08:00,160
These tea juices are what is going to create the oxidation

100
00:08:00,161 --> 00:08:04,920
in the leaf to turn it from green leaves into black tea.

101
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:08,560
The shredded leaves come out of the rotovane

102
00:08:08,561 --> 00:08:10,640
and onto the oxidation bed.

103
00:08:10,641 --> 00:08:13,040
Oxidation is the defining phase in the process

104
00:08:13,041 --> 00:08:15,000
of making black or oolong tea.

105
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:19,240
You can see it's a very rich green color.

106
00:08:19,241 --> 00:08:22,160
This tea is then gonna turn from green

107
00:08:22,161 --> 00:08:24,880
to a sort of coppery-orange color.

108
00:08:24,881 --> 00:08:27,700
This is where the miracle of tea takes place.

109
00:08:27,701 --> 00:08:29,720
This is where all the flavor is built.

110
00:08:29,721 --> 00:08:32,820
This is where most of the quality comes from.

111
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:40,100
Oxidation is the key to making black oolong or green tea.

112
00:08:40,101 --> 00:08:43,120
If the tea maker cuts the oxidation time in half,

113
00:08:43,121 --> 00:08:45,120
he'd have oolong, a lighter tea

114
00:08:45,121 --> 00:08:47,760
that is served in most Chinese restaurants.

115
00:08:47,761 --> 00:08:50,120
If he didn't allow any oxidation at all,

116
00:08:50,121 --> 00:08:51,740
he'd end up with green tea,

117
00:08:51,741 --> 00:08:54,060
the most popular form of tea in Japan,

118
00:08:54,061 --> 00:08:56,620
and familiar to anyone who's ever gone to a sushi bar.

119
00:08:58,420 --> 00:09:00,820
Oxidation is stopped by steaming the fresh leaves

120
00:09:00,821 --> 00:09:02,920
as soon as they've been harvested.

121
00:09:02,921 --> 00:09:05,680
As these tea makers from China are doing,

122
00:09:05,681 --> 00:09:08,000
heat neutralizes the enzyme that allows the tea

123
00:09:08,001 --> 00:09:09,680
to oxidize and change color.

124
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:14,380
After steaming, green tea goes through the same process

125
00:09:14,381 --> 00:09:18,220
of withering, shredding, and drying as blackened oolong tea.

126
00:09:19,580 --> 00:09:21,220
But today at Charleston,

127
00:09:21,221 --> 00:09:22,460
we're making black tea.

128
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:25,700
After exactly 50 minutes,

129
00:09:25,701 --> 00:09:28,160
these leaves will produce the kind of light bright tea

130
00:09:28,161 --> 00:09:30,360
that's favored in the US.

131
00:09:30,361 --> 00:09:33,340
This is the oxidation that we're looking for.

132
00:09:33,341 --> 00:09:37,720
This sort of coppery, orangey color inside the leaf

133
00:09:37,721 --> 00:09:40,060
that then goes into the dryer

134
00:09:40,061 --> 00:09:44,340
where we're going to take that 68% moisture

135
00:09:44,341 --> 00:09:47,060
and reduce it down to just 2%.

136
00:09:47,061 --> 00:09:54,060
Leaves are dried to exactly 2% moisture content because anything more would allow the tea to mold later on.

137
00:09:54,061 --> 00:09:59,060
The harvested leaf has now been reduced to 20% of its original size and weight.

138
00:09:59,061 --> 00:10:04,060
That's how 5 pounds of tea from the field make a single pound of tea.

139
00:10:04,061 --> 00:10:09,060
That pound now goes through a series of shaker sieves to remove impurities.

140
00:10:09,061 --> 00:10:14,060
A final sieve uses static electricity to pull out tiny bits of fiber.

141
00:10:14,061 --> 00:10:18,680
fiber. In most countries these pieces are painstakingly plucked by hand.

142
00:10:18,681 --> 00:10:25,680
These plastic rollers are pressed against felt and underneath that creates a static charge.

143
00:10:25,681 --> 00:10:33,320
As the black tea goes along the conveyor, all the stem and fiber is sucked up and taken

144
00:10:33,321 --> 00:10:34,809
out of the black tea.

145
00:10:34,810 --> 00:10:39,560
And this is what we've taken out of the tea to end up with the pure

146
00:10:39,561 --> 00:10:46,520
black tea ready to be packed into tea bags. The process of making tea which is

147
00:10:46,521 --> 00:10:51,560
basically the same around the world with variations in technology is simple but

148
00:10:51,561 --> 00:10:57,800
exacting. We have a saying in the tea world that tea is made in the field the

149
00:10:57,801 --> 00:11:03,320
quality is produced in the field and all you can do in the factory is ruin it or

150
00:11:03,321 --> 00:11:09,380
maintain that good quality. One can only speculate about the quality of the

151
00:11:09,381 --> 00:11:14,940
world's very first cup of tea.

152
00:11:14,941 --> 00:11:21,000
According to legend, the mythical Chinese emperor Shen Nung discovered tea in 2737

153
00:11:21,001 --> 00:11:24,265
BC when a couple of leaves fell into his drink.

154
00:11:24,266 --> 00:11:27,600
For over 3,000 years, China was the only country

155
00:11:27,601 --> 00:11:33,080
in the world that drank tea, and its impact on Chinese culture was enormous.

156
00:11:33,081 --> 00:11:35,768
To us, tea is more than just a beverage.

157
00:11:35,769 --> 00:11:38,860
It inspires poetry and painting and the art of

158
00:11:38,861 --> 00:11:43,900
Just making tea, Chinese people view as making connection with tea,

159
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:45,940
where you're bringing the tea back to life.

160
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:51,200
From the start, tea was perceived as a remarkably healthy drink.

161
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:55,200
Black tea has about 40 milligrams of caffeine per cup,

162
00:11:55,620 --> 00:11:57,180
about half as much as a cup of coffee.

163
00:11:58,200 --> 00:12:00,100
Its energizing qualities were duly noted,

164
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:04,520
especially by Buddhist monks who used tea to stay alert during meditation.

165
00:12:04,521 --> 00:12:11,520
In fact, tea remained a Chinese secret until a monk brought some tea seeds to Japan in

166
00:12:11,521 --> 00:12:12,699
the 9th century.

167
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:18,520
Building on Chinese traditions, the Japanese developed elaborate tea ceremonies

168
00:12:18,521 --> 00:12:22,520
that eventually became known as cha-do, the way of tea.

169
00:12:22,521 --> 00:12:25,402
Think of tea as Buddhist communion.

170
00:12:25,403 --> 00:12:29,520
We are more attuned to what we're paying attention

171
00:12:29,521 --> 00:12:32,720
and less attuned to distraction when we drink tea.

172
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:36,960
So it's a question of wakeful tranquility.

173
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:41,060
And that's exactly what Buddhism is trying to teach.

174
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:47,720
The Portuguese and the Dutch were the first to bring tea to Europe

175
00:12:47,721 --> 00:12:49,420
at the beginning of the 17th century.

176
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:52,400
Tea came late to England.

177
00:12:52,940 --> 00:12:57,000
But when it did finally arrive in 1664, it took off.

178
00:12:57,001 --> 00:13:02,680
King Charles' Portuguese wife, Catherine, had fallen in love with the exotic Chinese drink in Lisbon.

179
00:13:02,681 --> 00:13:05,480
And English aristocrats followed her lead.

180
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:09,920
For a long time, aristocrats were the only ones who could afford tea.

181
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:17,800
It was so heavily taxed, 119% in 1706, that it spawned an enormous black market.

182
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:25,020
By the 1770s, half of the tea in England, an estimated 7 million pounds a year, was illegal.

183
00:13:25,021 --> 00:13:31,520
Bootleg tea was heavily adulterated, filled with twigs and leaves, as well as toxic lead

184
00:13:31,521 --> 00:13:37,780
chromate, and even sheep's dung, both of which were used as colorizers.

185
00:13:37,781 --> 00:13:45,140
Smuggling ended when Prime Minister William Pitt finally slashed taxes in 1784.

186
00:13:45,141 --> 00:13:48,220
Legal tea was now affordable to everyone.

187
00:13:48,221 --> 00:13:54,900
But before that happened, there was a little matter involving tea in the American colonies.

188
00:13:54,901 --> 00:14:02,360
In late November and early December 1773, three ships loaded with 342 chests of tea reached

189
00:14:02,361 --> 00:14:04,860
Boston Harbor.

190
00:14:04,861 --> 00:14:09,260
Bostonians, increasingly touchy about the issue of taxation without representation,

191
00:14:09,261 --> 00:14:13,740
refused to let the ships unload.

192
00:14:13,741 --> 00:14:19,760
On the evening of December 16th, a group of 30 to 60 colonists disguised as Mohawks broke

193
00:14:19,761 --> 00:14:24,420
open the chests and dumped the tea in the harbor.

194
00:14:24,421 --> 00:14:28,380
The Boston Tea Party pushed relations between the British Crown and the colonies to the

195
00:14:28,381 --> 00:14:30,040
boiling point.

196
00:14:30,041 --> 00:14:34,160
In less than three years, the Revolutionary War was underway.

197
00:14:34,161 --> 00:14:38,780
And tea, which had been highly fashionable and popular throughout the colonies, was suddenly

198
00:14:38,781 --> 00:14:39,780
stigmatized.

199
00:14:39,781 --> 00:14:44,180
Tea becomes the ultimate symbol of British tyranny.

200
00:14:44,181 --> 00:14:46,720
So it becomes the thing that you despise.

201
00:14:46,721 --> 00:14:53,760
You have campaigns in the eastern United States to stop drinking tea in the drawing rooms.

202
00:14:53,761 --> 00:14:56,500
John Adams asked at a tavern,

203
00:14:56,900 --> 00:15:01,280
would it be lawful for a weary traveler to have a cup of tea,

204
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:03,940
provided it has been honestly smuggled?

205
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:06,200
The barmaid said, no, sir.

206
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:09,040
We have renounced tea under this roof.

207
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:11,060
I'll get you a cup of coffee.

208
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:16,900
Across the ocean in England, however, demand for tea continued to soar.

209
00:15:17,740 --> 00:15:22,280
To meet demand, the British would build plantations in India,

210
00:15:22,281 --> 00:15:28,500
tea plantations that would be modeled on the slave plantations of the American South.

211
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:34,160
The United States is the only country in the world that prefers its tea iced.

212
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:37,560
Eighty percent of all tea consumed in the U.S. is iced tea.

213
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:41,700
We now return to tea on Modern Marvels.

214
00:15:41,701 --> 00:15:55,701
The role of tea in the making and breaking of empires is inextricably bound up with one of the world's first and most powerful cartels, the British East India Trading

215
00:15:56,888 --> 00:15:57,620
Company.

216
00:15:58,980 --> 00:16:07,440
The so-called EIC was founded in 1600 by Queen Elizabeth I, who gave it a royal monopoly on all trade with the Far East.

217
00:16:07,441 --> 00:16:12,600
The East India Company would become the backbone of the emerging British Empire.

218
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:19,320
And tea, which eventually accounted for fully a third of its profits, would become the backbone of the EIC.

219
00:16:20,920 --> 00:16:28,800
By the start of the 19th century, tea was the third most heavily shipped commodity in the world, behind iron ore and cotton.

220
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:31,940
Britain wasn't the only empire fueled by tea.

221
00:16:31,941 --> 00:16:38,940
The Chinese had a global monopoly on selling tea, and they weren't interested in selling it for anything but silver.

222
00:16:38,941 --> 00:16:42,940
This led to a trade imbalance with England, known as the Silver Sink,

223
00:16:42,941 --> 00:16:47,940
which ultimately paved the way for one of the most shameful chapters in British history.

224
00:16:47,941 --> 00:16:50,940
The Opium Wars are about tea.

225
00:16:50,941 --> 00:16:58,940
The Opium Wars take place because the British start flooding southern China with Indian Opium,

226
00:16:58,941 --> 00:17:05,940
selling opium to local people in southern China to pay for the trade imbalance around the tea trade.

227
00:17:05,941 --> 00:17:09,940
And that really becomes the base for the two opium wars.

228
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:16,940
In other words, the British went to war with China twice to force the Chinese to buy British opium,

229
00:17:16,941 --> 00:17:21,940
so that the British and the East India Trading Company would have silver to pay for their tea.

230
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:30,140
Their entire enterprise rested on a product that went down the drain in Europe, which

231
00:17:30,141 --> 00:17:35,140
was purchased with another which went up in smoke in Asia, and that was opium.

232
00:17:35,141 --> 00:17:40,880
The trade imbalance with China and the fact that it lost its royal monopoly on trade with

233
00:17:40,881 --> 00:17:46,520
the Orient in 1834 prompted the East India Company and others to try to cultivate tea

234
00:17:46,521 --> 00:17:48,160
outside of China.

235
00:17:48,161 --> 00:17:53,600
An indigenous tea plant had been found in the Assam region of India in 1820.

236
00:17:54,100 --> 00:17:57,500
And in India, the East India Trading Company could do as it pleased.

237
00:17:58,160 --> 00:18:02,260
They could try you and execute you for violation of their own laws.

238
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:05,480
They had standing armies.

239
00:18:05,660 --> 00:18:09,160
They had a navy, which was larger than the British Royal Navy.

240
00:18:09,680 --> 00:18:13,260
So if you're talking about power, it's a power that's rarely been equaled.

241
00:18:14,060 --> 00:18:15,780
This was their colony.

242
00:18:15,781 --> 00:18:22,900
Therefore, they could move into areas and say, this is wasteland, we're going to bring these people into work, and that's it.

243
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:25,100
It's a done deal, because it's theirs.

244
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:32,480
With brutal haste, the British hacked an infrastructure through jungles and forests,

245
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:38,360
created massive plantations, and imported tens of thousands of workers to labor in the fields.

246
00:18:38,361 --> 00:18:44,460
These are systems of production and models of production that have been used in the United

247
00:18:44,461 --> 00:18:47,900
States and the Caribbean for 200 years.

248
00:18:47,901 --> 00:18:49,400
The slave plantation system.

249
00:18:49,401 --> 00:18:51,100
There's a big house.

250
00:18:51,101 --> 00:18:57,180
There is hundreds if not thousands of people who are harnessed to work on these systems

251
00:18:57,181 --> 00:19:00,420
with very little if not any pay.

252
00:19:00,421 --> 00:19:05,680
They are brought in from other places to do the work and they become a trapped indentured

253
00:19:05,681 --> 00:19:06,680
labor force.

254
00:19:06,681 --> 00:19:11,440
Plantation owners even used laws from antebellum slavery in the U.S.

255
00:19:11,441 --> 00:19:13,680
to keep its tea workers in line.

256
00:19:13,681 --> 00:19:18,680
If you as a worker tried to escape, you were tracked down like a fugitive.

257
00:19:18,681 --> 00:19:28,680
And the Fugitive Slave Law of America, which was used here in slave plantations, was actually applied by the British in tea country.

258
00:19:28,681 --> 00:19:34,680
The disconnect in England about what was happening in India around tea was profound.

259
00:19:34,681 --> 00:19:45,980
70% of the plantation workforce was female, and romanticized images of exotic women with nimble fingers became a staple of tea marketing that continues to this day.

260
00:19:47,880 --> 00:19:56,200
Meanwhile, afternoon tea, introduced by the Duchess of Bedford in 1840 to stave off hunger pains, became a national passion.

261
00:19:56,201 --> 00:20:03,940
Tea produced by virtual slaves, many of whom died under savage conditions in the field,

262
00:20:03,941 --> 00:20:09,240
now emerged as Britain's quintessential symbol of civility.

263
00:20:09,241 --> 00:20:15,440
By 1855, Assam was exporting over 500,000 pounds of tea.

264
00:20:15,441 --> 00:20:21,300
The number shot up to 86 million pounds within 30 years, with British imports surpassing those

265
00:20:21,301 --> 00:20:23,960
from China in 1888.

266
00:20:23,961 --> 00:20:29,120
Along the way, the economy and the landscape of much of the subcontinent was stunningly altered.

267
00:20:30,100 --> 00:20:35,260
Vast and uniform new tea fields were engineered to make it as easy as possible to harvest tea.

268
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:40,960
You see miles and miles and miles of the same height of tea bushes.

269
00:20:41,120 --> 00:20:43,420
It looks like a bonsai forest.

270
00:20:43,421 --> 00:20:53,900
And the kind of technical attention and focus during labor of keeping those bushes absolutely even is extraordinary.

271
00:20:54,240 --> 00:20:57,240
When you're looking at it from afar, it's like an optical illusion.

272
00:20:57,620 --> 00:21:03,440
And you think actually that it is like a green carpet, you know, that just stretches out.

273
00:21:05,060 --> 00:21:09,880
The geometric tea fields reflected a new regime of industrialized tea production.

274
00:21:10,780 --> 00:21:15,540
Every plantation had its own factory to process the massive harvests of raw leaf.

275
00:21:16,660 --> 00:21:20,480
The result was a fundamental shift from the Chinese model of tea manufacturing,

276
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:24,240
which featured small-scale production of many different kinds of tea.

277
00:21:25,460 --> 00:21:30,940
To make things as efficient as possible, the British decided to produce primarily black tea.

278
00:21:30,941 --> 00:21:39,940
British Empire tea which would be produced on similar plantations in Ceylon, Kenya and other colonies

279
00:21:39,941 --> 00:21:42,400
became synonymous with black tea

280
00:21:42,401 --> 00:21:48,080
and black tea outside of Japan and China became synonymous with tea

281
00:21:48,081 --> 00:21:55,160
it would take more than a century for the hundreds of varieties of handcrafted Chinese teas

282
00:21:55,161 --> 00:21:58,200
to once again find their way into the West

283
00:21:58,201 --> 00:22:05,200
In the meantime, mass-produced teas would generate new forms and new technologies.

284
00:22:05,201 --> 00:22:13,200
Tea pluckers in the Hatton district of central Sri Lanka typically earn about 125 rupees a day, or $1.20.

285
00:22:13,201 --> 00:22:17,200
We now return to tea on Modern Marvelous.

286
00:22:17,201 --> 00:22:18,040
Modern Marvels.

287
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:28,680
The tea bag didn't arrive until the 20th century,

288
00:22:28,681 --> 00:22:30,160
but it quickly established itself

289
00:22:30,161 --> 00:22:33,080
as one of the iconic everyday objects of modern life.

290
00:22:35,140 --> 00:22:37,520
Especially in the United States,

291
00:22:37,521 --> 00:22:40,820
where 65% of all tea, both iced and hot,

292
00:22:40,821 --> 00:22:42,160
is brewed through the bag.

293
00:22:42,161 --> 00:22:52,160
The Lipton Tea Company alone makes some 9 billion tea bags a year just in the U.S.,

294
00:22:52,161 --> 00:22:58,160
90% of them here in Lipton's blending and tea bagging plant in Suffolk, Virginia.

295
00:22:58,161 --> 00:23:03,160
Our Suffolk facility is one of the largest, if not the largest, tea bag facility in the world.

296
00:23:03,161 --> 00:23:10,160
And if you can imagine, about 15 or 20 40-foot ocean-going containers full of tea

297
00:23:10,161 --> 00:23:14,660
tea going through that plant each week that's the kind of volumes that we put

298
00:23:14,661 --> 00:23:17,480
through this facility

299
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:23,120
long rows of these frenetic machines punch out a million tea bags an hour

300
00:23:23,121 --> 00:23:28,360
24 million a day seven days a week

301
00:23:29,020 --> 00:23:34,560
the machines make around 200 bags a minute and use an astonishing 2,500

302
00:23:34,561 --> 00:23:39,800
moving parts to whip together the simple pouches it looks like an expensive clock

303
00:23:39,801 --> 00:23:43,160
that has all these moving parts and you're standing there and you're

304
00:23:43,161 --> 00:23:48,200
watching it and it's just fascinating to see all this stop-and-go movement in

305
00:23:48,201 --> 00:23:55,300
the creation of a teabag. Lipton teabagging components, paper, label, string and

306
00:23:55,301 --> 00:24:01,400
staples, fly through the machine with amazing precision and speed. The sides of

307
00:24:01,401 --> 00:24:06,080
the bags are not glued together but folded so neatly that they hold their

308
00:24:06,081 --> 00:24:07,840
shape when steeped in boiling water.

309
00:24:07,841 --> 00:24:13,460
The silicone bowl is very important because if it's not a tight bowl, the tea's gonna

310
00:24:13,461 --> 00:24:15,140
come out into the cup.

311
00:24:15,141 --> 00:24:19,060
And that's something we do not want.

312
00:24:19,061 --> 00:24:22,180
Teabags were an accidental revolution.

313
00:24:22,181 --> 00:24:27,360
An American tea seller sent out samples of tea in silk pouches in 1908.

314
00:24:27,361 --> 00:24:31,600
Instead of taking the tea out and brewing it loose leaf style, buyers just dropped the

315
00:24:31,601 --> 00:24:35,720
pouches in hot water and the teabag was born.

316
00:24:35,721 --> 00:24:38,520
Eventually, the silk became gauze, and then paper.

317
00:24:39,660 --> 00:24:43,020
Commercial production in the 1920s added the string and tag.

318
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:47,800
A big breakthrough in teabagging technology came in the 1950s,

319
00:24:47,960 --> 00:24:52,080
when the teabagging machines that Lipton still uses were first introduced.

320
00:24:52,980 --> 00:24:57,700
The machines were designed to create Lipton's signature double-sided, or flow-through bag.

321
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:02,620
If you put two teabags together, you'd get more tea flavor.

322
00:25:03,500 --> 00:25:06,300
Well, that's the idea behind our Lipton flow-through bag.

323
00:25:07,540 --> 00:25:11,380
And Lipton converted their entire operation to manufacturing that bag,

324
00:25:11,740 --> 00:25:15,000
and it vaulted Lipton by far to the number one position in the marketplace.

325
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:21,140
And the advantage of this teabag was that you actually created four sides for the tea to be exposed to water,

326
00:25:21,320 --> 00:25:26,760
and it created more space in the teabag for the leaves to expand and to brew properly.

327
00:25:26,761 --> 00:25:35,880
Lipton's newest generation of teabagging machines is three times as fast as the older models.

328
00:25:35,881 --> 00:25:41,000
This one, which shoots out 600 bags a minute, is used to bag Lipton's green and specialty

329
00:25:41,001 --> 00:25:49,580
tees.

330
00:25:49,581 --> 00:25:54,640
The technology, meanwhile, is racing to keep up with the changes in the traditional teabag,

331
00:25:54,641 --> 00:25:59,100
Like this single serve stick from the Serengeti Tea Company in Los Angeles.

332
00:25:59,101 --> 00:26:05,980
And roomier pyramid bags that can hold larger pieces of leaf.

333
00:26:05,981 --> 00:26:11,640
Shooting tea into small pouches is the culmination of a long process for mass market tea manufacturers

334
00:26:11,641 --> 00:26:15,280
like Lipton.

335
00:26:15,281 --> 00:26:18,920
Tea tasting is at the heart of that process.

336
00:26:18,921 --> 00:26:22,960
Yep, that's okay.

337
00:26:22,961 --> 00:26:32,380
That's because most Lipton teas are actually blends made up of dozens of individual teas.

338
00:26:32,381 --> 00:26:37,720
Making sure those blends have consistent flavor, color and mouthfeel requires constant changes

339
00:26:37,721 --> 00:26:41,300
in the formula and constant tasting.

340
00:26:41,301 --> 00:26:47,400
The reason we have to keep tasting to maintain that taste is every tea garden can make a

341
00:26:47,401 --> 00:26:48,960
different type of tea every week.

342
00:26:48,961 --> 00:26:55,460
The quality of the teas can change according to weather, according to how well they plucked the leaf and how well it was rolled.

343
00:26:55,600 --> 00:26:57,494
So it's almost like a juggling act.

344
00:26:57,495 --> 00:27:00,740
You're constantly changing the formulas to keep consistency.

345
00:27:02,320 --> 00:27:07,620
Lipton's classic orange peco tea is made up of between 40 and 60 teas.

346
00:27:18,960 --> 00:27:27,580
Lipton has devised a numerical code from 1 to 7 that it uses to describe the attributes of tees.

347
00:27:28,440 --> 00:27:34,320
So for our mainstream blend, we try and keep around a taste score of, you know, say 4.4.

348
00:27:34,640 --> 00:27:40,380
We try and keep a very high color or hue score of maybe 6.8.

349
00:27:40,740 --> 00:27:46,380
And we'd probably try and keep a medium mouthfeeler thickness score of around 4.8.

350
00:27:46,620 --> 00:27:47,960
It's fairly thick, so...

351
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:50,520
About a 5.2.

352
00:27:52,480 --> 00:27:56,740
Tea tasting is a skill that requires four to five years of intense training.

353
00:27:57,900 --> 00:28:00,300
John Cheatham served his apprenticeship in Sri Lanka,

354
00:28:00,600 --> 00:28:03,420
where he tasted as many as 1,200 teas a day.

355
00:28:04,060 --> 00:28:06,660
It's a bit like learning to play a musical instrument.

356
00:28:07,180 --> 00:28:09,340
The more you practice, the better you get at it.

357
00:28:09,800 --> 00:28:14,080
And the more you practice, the more you're better to pick out different types of tastes.

358
00:28:14,081 --> 00:28:19,180
and in the end you're able to pick out the best teas that will fit in the Lipton blends.

359
00:28:19,181 --> 00:28:25,900
When we're tasting we suck in the tea at around 100 miles per hour and that's so you can

360
00:28:25,901 --> 00:28:31,900
oxidize the tea and break it up into particles and also spray it around to different parts of

361
00:28:31,901 --> 00:28:35,200
your mouth because you're tasting different things with different parts of your mouth.

362
00:28:35,201 --> 00:28:41,260
Lipton's ever-changing blend sheets are programmed at company headquarters in

363
00:28:41,261 --> 00:28:46,600
in Anglewood Cliffs, New Jersey, and then downloaded into the computerized blending

364
00:28:46,601 --> 00:28:49,700
system at Suffolk.

365
00:28:49,701 --> 00:28:55,740
Vast quantities of tea from around the world are processed here every day, vigorously sifted

366
00:28:55,741 --> 00:29:00,020
to remove impurities, and then funneled into massive silos.

367
00:29:00,021 --> 00:29:06,220
A typical silo here will hold about 10,000 pounds, and we'll go through that in a 24-hour

368
00:29:06,221 --> 00:29:10,660
period, and we have multiple silos on site.

369
00:29:10,661 --> 00:29:16,660
Some silos hold just a single tea, while others hold a pre-blend of three to four teas.

370
00:29:16,661 --> 00:29:21,860
The silos themselves initiate Lipton's blending process by swirling the teas together as they

371
00:29:21,861 --> 00:29:25,800
fall through the conical end.

372
00:29:25,801 --> 00:29:30,260
Conveyor belts carry the teas from the silos to this blending machine, which gently tumbles

373
00:29:30,261 --> 00:29:33,440
the day's recipe.

374
00:29:33,441 --> 00:29:43,160
The blends are then conveyed to 2,000-pound super sacks that feed the tea bagging machines.

375
00:29:43,161 --> 00:29:46,440
Not all the tea in America is processed into tea bags.

376
00:29:46,441 --> 00:29:51,660
About one-sixth of it is converted into powder, which is used to make instant tea and also

377
00:29:51,661 --> 00:29:55,360
as part of fountain and bottled tea blends.

378
00:29:55,361 --> 00:30:00,020
Lippin and other major manufacturers produce and sell $400 million worth of tea powder

379
00:30:00,021 --> 00:30:04,160
each year.

380
00:30:04,161 --> 00:30:08,880
The powder is made in processing plants that brew tea in a large vat, then pump it up and

381
00:30:08,881 --> 00:30:14,940
spray it into the top of a tower that can rise up 100 feet or more.

382
00:30:14,941 --> 00:30:18,940
High temperatures in the upper part of the tower instantly vaporize the water in the

383
00:30:18,941 --> 00:30:27,080
tea, leaving a tea residue which crystallizes as it descends in the cooler air.

384
00:30:27,081 --> 00:30:32,620
Tea powder and tea bags are frowned upon in much of the world, including China and India,

385
00:30:32,621 --> 00:30:36,680
where many people still drink tea loose leaf style.

386
00:30:36,681 --> 00:30:43,520
Traditional drinkers insist that the directly infused leaf puts more flavor in the brew.

387
00:30:43,521 --> 00:30:46,980
But sometimes you can have too much of a good thing.

388
00:30:46,981 --> 00:30:53,240
Welcome to Celestial Seasonings and the overwhelming power of peppermint.

389
00:30:53,241 --> 00:30:59,860
Darjeeling tea grown in the foothills of the Himalayas is a light and subtle tea that can retail for hundreds of dollars a pound.

390
00:31:00,300 --> 00:31:02,220
It is known as the champagne of tea.

391
00:31:03,100 --> 00:31:06,240
We now return to tea on Modern Marvels.

392
00:31:10,760 --> 00:31:19,520
Every year, Celestial Seasonings uses half a million pounds of freshly picked peppermint and spearmint in its popular herb tea blends.

393
00:31:19,521 --> 00:31:27,380
This is where it's stored, in the mint room at the company warehouse and factory in Boulder, Colorado.

394
00:31:29,460 --> 00:31:31,420
The mint room is very intense.

395
00:31:31,840 --> 00:31:35,680
It'll go to your sinuses, your nose will start dripping, and your eyes will start watering.

396
00:31:35,880 --> 00:31:39,000
Some people can just stick their head in, and then they have to leave.

397
00:31:39,380 --> 00:31:43,580
In fact, a very popular T-shirt we have for sale is, I survived the mint room.

398
00:31:43,720 --> 00:31:49,400
We had to double seal the walls and the ceilings of this because the oil was so high.

399
00:31:49,520 --> 00:31:54,440
that it would leak through and contaminate the rest of the herbs so everything would be tasting like mint.

400
00:31:59,880 --> 00:32:06,060
Mint is just one of over a hundred herbs that Celestial Seasoning stocks in mega quantities for its various blends.

401
00:32:06,980 --> 00:32:10,440
A dog might go insane wandering these warehouse aisles,

402
00:32:10,700 --> 00:32:16,340
stuffed with everything from cinnamon to ginger to vanilla bean to licorice to lemongrass.

403
00:32:16,341 --> 00:32:24,000
The herb tea giant revolutionized the American tea industry in the 1970s

404
00:32:24,001 --> 00:32:29,080
with the introduction of feel-good botanical blends, like Sleepy Time and Red Zinger.

405
00:32:30,300 --> 00:32:33,000
Many of the blends were made entirely of herbs.

406
00:32:33,600 --> 00:32:36,100
They didn't contain Camellia sinensis, or tea.

407
00:32:36,740 --> 00:32:39,680
And they introduced consumers to a medicine chest of plants

408
00:32:39,681 --> 00:32:43,960
that could reputedly do everything from prevent a cold and soothe a sore throat

409
00:32:43,961 --> 00:32:47,200
to fire up your sex drive and settle your nerves.

410
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:53,060
Hibiscus, the herb that puts the zing in Red Zinger,

411
00:32:53,440 --> 00:32:55,740
is imported mainly from Thailand and China.

412
00:32:56,760 --> 00:32:59,780
Celestial Seasonings uses one million pounds of it a year.

413
00:33:01,520 --> 00:33:03,760
Before it can be blended with other ingredients,

414
00:33:04,320 --> 00:33:08,060
the raw compacted hibiscus, like all the other herbs that Celestial uses,

415
00:33:08,740 --> 00:33:12,540
has to be broken down into pieces that will fit into a tea bag.

416
00:33:12,541 --> 00:33:15,540
The first phase of milling is done by hand.

417
00:33:15,541 --> 00:33:20,540
What that individual is doing is breaking it down into consumable units, if you will,

418
00:33:20,541 --> 00:33:24,540
so that it doesn't back up within the internal mechanisms of the machine.

419
00:33:24,541 --> 00:33:26,540
That individual is also doing a visual inspection of the ingredients,

420
00:33:26,541 --> 00:33:29,540
so any form material that would be present in the ingredient

421
00:33:29,541 --> 00:33:33,540
before we go through our process, we're pulling that out.

422
00:33:33,541 --> 00:33:37,540
The herb is further broken down into smaller but still coarse pieces

423
00:33:37,541 --> 00:33:43,000
and then conveyed to a cutter mill where the real chopping is done.

424
00:33:44,560 --> 00:33:52,100
Here, the hibiscus falls onto a shaft embedded with seven 3-inch high and 1-inch thick blades that rotate at high speed.

425
00:33:54,160 --> 00:34:00,420
The shredded hibiscus is then sucked through the system into a gyrating sifter that holds 10 screens,

426
00:34:00,980 --> 00:34:03,480
each one with a finer mesh than the one before.

427
00:34:07,540 --> 00:34:11,260
We want it to be real consistent because if we have large particles and smaller particles,

428
00:34:11,680 --> 00:34:14,120
the infusion rate's going to be different for all of those particles.

429
00:34:16,380 --> 00:34:21,460
After milling, the herbs are blended and then funneled to Celestial's teabagging machines.

430
00:34:25,860 --> 00:34:29,520
Because these teabags don't have strings or tags or staples,

431
00:34:30,160 --> 00:34:32,840
these machines are a lot faster than those at Lipton,

432
00:34:33,560 --> 00:34:35,760
flinging out upwards of a thousand teabags a minute.

433
00:34:35,761 --> 00:34:42,060
It's a pretty intricate web that goes through the machine that will seal the two sides of

434
00:34:42,061 --> 00:34:44,720
the tea bag paper that actually has adhesive together.

435
00:34:44,721 --> 00:34:50,780
The sealing temperature is quite high, upwards of about 150 degrees centigrade, and so that's

436
00:34:50,781 --> 00:34:55,040
important to us because a consumer is putting boiling water on top of a tea bag at 100 degrees

437
00:34:55,041 --> 00:34:56,040
centigrade.

438
00:34:56,041 --> 00:34:59,440
We want to make sure that that bond is going to stay intact throughout that entire cup

439
00:34:59,441 --> 00:35:02,440
of tea.

440
00:35:02,441 --> 00:35:08,880
Celestial Seasonings pioneered the herb or hippie tea revolution in the United States in the late 1960s.

441
00:35:11,040 --> 00:35:15,060
The first herbs were picked in the wild from fields and meadows around Boulder.

442
00:35:16,560 --> 00:35:19,500
Weeds for needs was one of the company's early slogans.

443
00:35:20,700 --> 00:35:23,940
They wildcrafted it right here in the Rocky Mountains,

444
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:28,720
and then they had to figure a way to dry it and a place to dry it in so it wouldn't be rained on.

445
00:35:29,040 --> 00:35:32,080
So they used somebody's barn and another person's garage.

446
00:35:32,440 --> 00:35:40,980
And then they stuffed it into muslin bags by hand and put little strings around it and then took it to natural food stores.

447
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:49,380
Early reaction from the tea industry, which revolved almost entirely around traditional black tea, was dismissive.

448
00:35:49,860 --> 00:35:54,040
I remember going to a tea company in the south of London, England,

449
00:35:54,041 --> 00:36:00,220
and their manufacturing manager allowed me to tour their whole facility saying,

450
00:36:00,221 --> 00:36:02,160
you're no competition to us.

451
00:36:02,580 --> 00:36:05,160
And I think they take a little differently now.

452
00:36:06,720 --> 00:36:12,680
Indeed, herbal and flavored teas now account for over a third of teabag and loose leaf sales in America.

453
00:36:14,140 --> 00:36:18,560
Celestial Seasonings alone sells over $100 million worth of blends a year,

454
00:36:18,920 --> 00:36:20,920
most of them entirely herbal.

455
00:36:22,160 --> 00:36:25,700
But one botanical with great taste and remarkable medicinal value

456
00:36:25,701 --> 00:36:29,000
remains the original Camellia sinensis, or tea.

457
00:36:30,220 --> 00:36:32,980
It's been valued as a healthy potion for millennia.

458
00:36:33,620 --> 00:36:39,680
And new research indicates that it might turn out to be a pharmacological treasure trove of health benefits and cures.

459
00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:46,160
Which may explain why tea is finally giving its old rival, coffee, a run for the money.

460
00:36:47,680 --> 00:36:51,560
By excluding string, tags, and overwrap on its teabags,

461
00:36:51,880 --> 00:36:57,120
Celestial Seasonings eliminates 3,617,000 pounds of waste each year.

462
00:36:57,121 --> 00:37:02,120
We now return to tea on Modern Marvels.

463
00:37:02,121 --> 00:37:08,120
Put down that cup of coffee, it's time to spill the beans, and it's time to pick up

464
00:37:08,121 --> 00:37:11,120
a cup of good health, one cup of tea at a time.

465
00:37:11,121 --> 00:37:17,120
That's the mantra in trendy tea places like the Botanical Urban Tea Garden in Beverly

466
00:37:17,121 --> 00:37:18,120
Hills.

467
00:37:18,121 --> 00:37:24,120
Tea consumption doubled in the U.S. between 2001 and 2006.

468
00:37:24,121 --> 00:37:26,980
and more and more people are making tea their drink of choice.

469
00:37:27,780 --> 00:37:30,740
That's largely because of the reputed health benefits of tea.

470
00:37:31,780 --> 00:37:35,480
Research suggests that tea plays a role in the prevention of obesity,

471
00:37:36,120 --> 00:37:39,480
osteoporosis, gum disease, and heart disease,

472
00:37:40,020 --> 00:37:44,680
especially green tea, which was nearly impossible to find in the U.S. 10 years ago,

473
00:37:45,160 --> 00:37:47,600
and now accounts for 19% of the market.

474
00:37:48,860 --> 00:37:53,520
Green tea is packed with antioxidants that many researchers say prevents cell damage

475
00:37:53,521 --> 00:37:55,660
and slow down the aging process.

476
00:37:56,240 --> 00:37:58,900
But the number one antioxidant in green tea,

477
00:37:59,520 --> 00:38:04,840
a single molecule called EGCG, or epigallocatechin gallate,

478
00:38:05,280 --> 00:38:06,880
may have other beneficial properties.

479
00:38:07,420 --> 00:38:12,220
We were exploring chemicals that had been derived from Chinese herbal medicine

480
00:38:12,221 --> 00:38:17,340
and asking whether any of these chemicals could attack cancer-causing proteins.

481
00:38:17,840 --> 00:38:21,600
And sure enough, for one of the cancer targets that we were very interested in,

482
00:38:21,601 --> 00:38:27,920
we found this chemical from green tea that binds very tightly to this cancer protein

483
00:38:27,921 --> 00:38:28,980
and inhibits its activity.

484
00:38:31,140 --> 00:38:37,560
EGCG, which makes up about 70% of the dry weight of tea, neutralizes BCLXL,

485
00:38:38,120 --> 00:38:43,800
otherwise known as the anti-death protein, a gene that doesn't allow cancer cells to die.

486
00:38:44,280 --> 00:38:49,300
The anti-death genes become hyperactive, and this allows the cancer cell to expand,

487
00:38:49,301 --> 00:38:53,220
to increase its numbers because they've forgotten how to die.

488
00:38:53,620 --> 00:38:57,700
The same mechanism explains why it's often very difficult to kill cancer cells

489
00:38:57,701 --> 00:39:01,780
even with the best weapons we have, such as chemotherapy and radiation.

490
00:39:03,900 --> 00:39:09,200
Researchers used nuclear magnetic resonance, an advanced version of medicine's MRI,

491
00:39:09,860 --> 00:39:13,100
to get a picture of how a single molecule of EGCG

492
00:39:13,101 --> 00:39:16,820
interacts with a single molecule of the anti-death protein family.

493
00:39:16,821 --> 00:39:21,820
Dr. Maurizio Pallecchia was in charge of the research.

494
00:39:21,821 --> 00:39:28,820
This is the protein that when over-expressed in cancer cells prevent them from dying and allow them to proliferate.

495
00:39:28,821 --> 00:39:32,820
In red here we have this small molecule.

496
00:39:32,821 --> 00:39:37,820
This is a picallocatechin gallate, which even being very small compared with the protein,

497
00:39:37,821 --> 00:39:45,820
is able to insert itself on this deep crevice on the surface of the molecule and block its activity.

498
00:39:45,821 --> 00:39:52,620
Conclusive research on the cancer-killing benefits of green tea remains to be done,

499
00:39:52,621 --> 00:39:57,460
and it's unclear how much EGCG is actually in the kind of processed green teas on sale

500
00:39:57,461 --> 00:39:59,260
in most stores.

501
00:39:59,261 --> 00:40:05,320
Still, Dr. Reed is convinced that a little green tea every day can't hurt.

502
00:40:05,321 --> 00:40:11,140
You've got antioxidants, you've got the detoxifying effect of the chemicals revving up our own

503
00:40:11,141 --> 00:40:15,500
detoxifying systems, and then you've got the direct effect against cancer-causing proteins

504
00:40:15,501 --> 00:40:17,660
So why not take a chance?

505
00:40:19,440 --> 00:40:23,960
Which is exactly what people are doing at places like Dr. T's Urban Tea Garden.

506
00:40:24,820 --> 00:40:27,340
There are over 100 varieties of tea here.

507
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:30,380
Green as well as black, oolong and herb.

508
00:40:31,020 --> 00:40:34,240
Most of them imported from India and China in small batches.

509
00:40:37,100 --> 00:40:39,800
Unlike mass-produced teas which aim for consistency,

510
00:40:40,560 --> 00:40:42,580
the very inconsistency of these teas,

511
00:40:42,581 --> 00:40:48,200
the fact that they vary from harvest to harvest depending on climate, soil, and even the style of plucking

512
00:40:48,201 --> 00:40:50,940
is what makes them intriguing to devotees.

513
00:40:53,620 --> 00:40:54,760
And pricey.

514
00:40:55,700 --> 00:41:00,060
Dr. T's menu ranges from $4 to $300 a pot.

515
00:41:01,540 --> 00:41:05,860
This tea, called Taiping from the Fujian province of southern China,

516
00:41:06,460 --> 00:41:08,380
was once reserved for Chinese royalty

517
00:41:08,381 --> 00:41:13,500
and was served to Richard Nixon during his historic visit to China in 1972.

518
00:41:13,980 --> 00:41:22,500
This plantation produces, it's in its entire year, 150 kilos, 300 pounds of tea, and that's it.

519
00:41:22,800 --> 00:41:24,520
I was given 14 ounces.

520
00:41:25,200 --> 00:41:26,940
One of my most prized teas right here.

521
00:41:29,020 --> 00:41:32,200
Dr. T's most prized tea, however, is this one.

522
00:41:33,180 --> 00:41:38,360
It's Pu-erh, a category of Chinese tea that, like wine, improves with age.

523
00:41:38,380 --> 00:41:45,440
That's because of a processing method that's been a Chinese state secret since the 14th century

524
00:41:45,441 --> 00:41:48,800
which enables the leaf to develop a mold as it ages

525
00:41:48,801 --> 00:41:52,380
and that mold protects the tea and ripens the flavor

526
00:41:52,381 --> 00:41:59,860
You can see here how the blue is that little bacteria that grows on the tea cake

527
00:41:59,861 --> 00:42:06,080
and it's that bacteria combined with the chemical pH balance of the tea

528
00:42:06,081 --> 00:42:10,840
that provides this tea cake with the most delicate nose,

529
00:42:11,740 --> 00:42:15,920
the most delicate palate of sweetness that you have never experienced.

530
00:42:18,160 --> 00:42:19,260
Oh, my.

531
00:42:20,220 --> 00:42:24,220
Puerhs have sold at auction for as much as $40,000 per cake.

532
00:42:25,240 --> 00:42:30,080
Dr. T's 1952 Puerh sells for nearly $1,000 an ounce.

533
00:42:31,520 --> 00:42:34,800
But tea aficionados may not entirely feel the pain

534
00:42:34,801 --> 00:42:37,300
because tea, according to Dr. T,

535
00:42:37,900 --> 00:42:40,780
is the only plant on Earth that has a stimulant, caffeine,

536
00:42:41,340 --> 00:42:44,600
and a tranquilizer, something called L-theanine.

537
00:42:45,260 --> 00:42:48,460
That makes the tea buzz totally different from that of coffee.

538
00:42:49,060 --> 00:42:51,060
The caffeine stimulates the beta brainwaves,

539
00:42:51,061 --> 00:42:52,960
the fright-flight response.

540
00:42:53,360 --> 00:42:55,780
Your body goes into a state of shock with caffeine.

541
00:42:56,340 --> 00:42:58,780
It's ready to take care of it in a perilous situation.

542
00:42:59,800 --> 00:43:03,360
L-theanine secreted into the brain stimulates the alpha brainwaves,

543
00:43:03,361 --> 00:43:07,220
relaxed, focused, meditative energy.

544
00:43:07,880 --> 00:43:10,040
Tea has it, coffee does not.

545
00:43:10,560 --> 00:43:12,900
No pleasure is simpler,

546
00:43:13,980 --> 00:43:15,780
no luxury cheaper,

547
00:43:16,820 --> 00:43:20,140
and no consciousness-altering substance

548
00:43:20,141 --> 00:43:24,280
more benign than our simple tea.

549
00:43:24,900 --> 00:43:28,000
You want to be ceremonious about tea making?

550
00:43:28,240 --> 00:43:29,780
There's certainly tea for you.

551
00:43:30,300 --> 00:43:32,240
You want something to cringe your thirst?

552
00:43:32,241 --> 00:43:33,480
has tea for you.

553
00:43:34,020 --> 00:43:36,860
You want something to keep you up working,

554
00:43:37,000 --> 00:43:37,880
that's tea for you.

555
00:43:38,260 --> 00:43:40,960
There's nothing else on earth like it.

556
00:43:42,880 --> 00:43:46,860
Which may explain why the 5,000-year-old drink of emperors

557
00:43:46,861 --> 00:43:49,560
has become the preferred drink of humankind.

558
00:43:51,640 --> 00:43:55,380
Not only does tea have a unique ability to wake up and calm.

559
00:43:56,420 --> 00:44:00,040
Like wine, the best teas have an extraordinary range

560
00:44:00,041 --> 00:44:01,300
and subtlety of tastes.

561
00:44:02,240 --> 00:44:08,040
Whether tea time represents a moment of tranquility in the stampede of life, or an inexpensive

562
00:44:08,041 --> 00:44:14,480
epicurean thrill, the human thirst for this benign and inscrutable drink promises to deepen

563
00:44:14,481 --> 00:44:15,900
for millennia to come.

